sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Formalize Smith's proof and bound the computational power of its initial tape" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3> ;
    dct:description "Machine-check what is currently claimed, with the definitional issue stated exactly. Formalize in Lean 4 Smith's theorem: for every CTS there is a computable initial tape on which the (2,3) machine emulates it. Then prove a theorem about the tape's complexity, which is what the dispute is about. Candidate statements: the tape is generated by a fixed finite-state transducer from the CTS description and a fixed infinite word; it is a fixed morphic or automatic sequence modified by a finite prefix; or it is computable in linear time by a non-universal device. Each turns 'the encoding smuggles in computation' into a checkable claim. If the tape is automatic or morphic, the result places the (2,3) machine in a precisely named universality class between weak universality (periodic background) and Smith's notion." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A Lean module proving Smith's theorem together with a proved upper bound on the complexity of the required tape (for example 'the tape is k-automatic'). That settles the definitional dispute in the form 'universal under definition D', even though it leaves the periodic-background case open." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Formalization uncovers a gap in Smith's chain that cannot be closed, or the tape generator turns out to need unbounded computation in a way that makes every weak-class statement false." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Formalize the (2,3) machine, CTS and Smith's intermediate systems in Lean; prove each emulation step. 2. Extract the explicit initial-tape generator from the proof and formalize it as a function. 3. Prove the generator lies in a named weak class (finite transducer over a fixed sequence, automatic sequence, or linear-time and non-universal), or show it cannot, by exhibiting an encoding step that requires unbounded computation. 4. Write a short mapping of the critics' objections onto the formal statements." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Smith's chain is long (about 40 pages of construction); the full formalization is likely beyond a month, though the tape-complexity analysis can be done on the informal proof first." ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: mathlib" ;
    skos:note "Tool: computability-theory" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-27> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-28> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-23> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.29.1.1> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2008.09.051> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Settle whether the (2,3) Turing machine is universal under a conventional definition" .


  <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.29.1.1> dct:title "Universality of Wolfram’s 2, 3 Turing Machine" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Smith 2020, the proof to formalize" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2008.09.051> dct:title "The complexity of small universal Turing machines: A survey" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Woods and Neary 2009, universality notions" .
  <https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4> dct:bibliographicCitation "Mathlib.Computability: Turing machine models, partial recursive functions" .
}

